{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates whether each lottery contains a 'sure outcome'—that is, an outcome that occurs with 100% probability—and prefers the lottery that does *not* have such a sure outcome. In Lottery A, there is a 90% chance of -3000 Euros and 10% chance of 0 Euros; no outcome occurs with 100% probability, so there is no sure outcome. In Lottery B, there is a 45% chance of -6000 Euros and 55% chance of 0 Euros; again, no outcome occurs with 100% probability, so there is also no sure outcome. Therefore, both lotteries lack a sure outcome. According to the decision reason, since both lotteries are equally lacking in sure outcomes, the reason would be indifferent between them. The relevant information (presence or absence of a sure outcome) can be derived from the problem, but in this case, the condition for preference (one having a sure outcome, the other not) does not apply because neither has a sure outcome.",
